"""Additional original instruction for the Operable curriculum generator."""

EXPANDED_NOTES = {
    '2.1.1': [
        'A keyboard interface is a way of giving commands through keystrokes; the person may use a physical keyboard, a switch device that sends keyboard events, or another assistive technology. To assess an action, separate reaching its control from executing it and completing any resulting workflow. For instance, reaching an Export button is only the beginning if its file-format dialog, confirmation, or download control requires a mouse. A component that accepts focus but does not respond to an expected key is still a barrier.',
        'Prefer the element that already expresses the intended operation: use a button to perform an action and a link to navigate. These elements supply useful browser behavior, but they do not fix a surrounding workflow automatically. For a custom composite widget, consult the interaction pattern appropriate to that widget and provide the expected movement and activation keys. Do not indiscriminately add every item to the tab order or intercept Tab when the page needs it for ordinary movement.',
        'The path-dependent exception refers to the nature of the underlying function. Freehand painting depends on the path traced, whereas selecting a menu item does not. A control does not become exempt just because its author implemented selection through a gesture. During review, list each operation, the keystrokes used to reach and perform it, the resulting state, and any blocked step. Test in both directions and with no time pressure; do not confuse this baseline exception with the stricter AAA 2.1.3 requirement.'
    ],
    '2.1.2': [
        'Focus indicates where keyboard input is directed. A trap occurs when a person can move into a component but cannot move away through a keyboard interface. The impact can extend beyond the component: the user may be unable to reach the form, browser controls, or other page content without abandoning or reloading the task. A focus loop inside a modal is different if the person can close the modal using the keyboard and continue afterward.',
        'Test the entry, interaction, and exit of each component. Tab into a date picker, use its arrow keys to select a date, and then try Tab, Shift+Tab, Escape, and its close control as appropriate to the widget. If a nonstandard exit method is necessary, provide instructions before the user needs them. A visible Close button is useful only if focus can reach it and its keyboard activation works.',
        'After closing a dialog, returning focus to the control that opened it often preserves the user’s context. If that control no longer exists, move focus to another logical location and explain the outcome where appropriate. Embedded content and third-party widgets deserve separate testing because their event handlers may consume keys differently from the host page. Record the exact keys and focus position when reporting a trap so a developer can reproduce it.'
    ],
    '2.2.1': [
        'A time limit imposed by content can affect someone who reads slowly, uses a screen reader, needs a break, or navigates through many fields with a switch. The practical problem is not the presence of a clock by itself: it is that the task, session, or opportunity ends before the person can complete it. Identify each deadline separately, including form expiry, quiz deadlines, temporary verification codes, and automatically refreshed content.',
        'The criterion offers alternatives with precise conditions. A user can turn off the limit before encountering it, adjust it before encountering it to at least ten times the default, or receive a warning before expiry that provides at least 20 seconds for a simple extension and permits at least ten extensions. The applicable exceptions have their own conditions, including real-time, essential, and longer-than-20-hour situations. A preferred business rule is not automatically essential.',
        'For an extension design, start a test session with a shortened but representative timeout. Record when the warning appears, whether focus remains manageable, and whether one simple action extends the same session. Repeat the extension at least ten times and check that entered data stays intact. Test the turn-off or adjustment path before starting the timed activity if the product chooses one of those alternatives. Separately evaluate reauthentication and data-loss requirements when expiry can still occur.'
    ],
    '2.4.3': [
        'Sequential focus order is the path followed by Tab and Shift+Tab. It matters when the sequence affects meaning or operation: a person should encounter a control in a context that makes sense and be able to complete a task without unexpected jumps. The sequence need not match visual placement in every situation, but discrepancies that change the meaning or break the workflow must be corrected.',
        'Start with document order. CSS can visually rearrange items without changing their position in the DOM, and positive tabindex values can create a separate sequence that is difficult to maintain. Rather than assigning arbitrary positive numbers, arrange the source in a meaningful order and use normal focus behavior. Check layouts at narrow widths, where columns may stack in a different order, and check dynamically inserted controls such as validation messages.',
        'When a dialog opens, send focus to an appropriate element inside it so the user knows the context has changed. While the dialog is active, manage its focus according to its interaction pattern, and provide a usable way to close it. After closing, restore focus to a logical location, usually the opener. Test forward and backward movement before, inside, and after the dialog; inspect the behavior when the opener has been removed or the form has reported errors.'
    ],
    '2.4.7': [
        'The focus indicator tells a keyboard user which control will receive the next command. It is different from the mouse hover state and from the selected state of a tab or menu item. An interface can have a selected navigation item while focus is on another link, so both states must remain understandable. Removing the browser outline without a suitable replacement makes users guess where they are.',
        'Use a visible style for keyboard focus on links, buttons, form fields, and custom controls. The :focus-visible selector can help distinguish keyboard-style focus from pointer interactions, but verify behavior in the browsers and components the product supports. A border that resembles the normal border, appears only on hover, or is covered by a neighboring element is not a useful indicator. Consider forced-colors and high-contrast settings as well as ordinary themes.',
        'Walk through an entire task with Tab and Shift+Tab, watching the focused control at every step. Check on light and dark backgrounds, inside dialogs, and when the page is zoomed. Ask whether the indicator moves with focus and remains visible over both the component and surrounding surface. This criterion requires a visible mode of operation; the distinct AAA focus appearance criterion specifies additional measurements and exceptions.'
    ],
    '2.4.11': [
        'A component can receive keyboard focus while a sticky header, footer, banner, or chat panel covers it. The user may press Enter or type without seeing which element has focus. This AA criterion addresses a focused component that is entirely hidden by author-created content; the related AAA criterion requires the whole component to remain unobscured. The component and its focus indicator are related but separate things to inspect.',
        'Reproduce the problem with a short viewport and at browser zoom. Tab to controls near a fixed header and footer, then examine whether any part of the focused component is visible. An overlay that only appears at a breakpoint or after a notification is opened can make the problem intermittent. Also check dynamically opened menus and the layout when a virtual keyboard reduces the available viewport.',
        'Potential repairs include reserving space for fixed regions, repositioning them, or setting suitable scroll offsets. A fixed offset can fail when the banner text wraps, so test its actual height across screen sizes and translated content. Repeat the keyboard sequence after the fix, and ensure the remedy does not hide a different control. Record the viewport, zoom, overlay state, and focused element to make a failure reproducible.'
    ],
    '2.5.1': [
        'A multipoint gesture requires more than one point of contact, such as a two-finger pinch. A path-based gesture depends on the path followed, such as a swipe. Some users cannot maintain multiple contacts or move a pointer along a particular route, even if they can activate a single target. This criterion generally requires an alternative that can be operated by a single pointer without a path, unless the gesture is essential.',
        'In an image gallery, previous and next buttons can provide the same navigation as a swipe. In a map, zoom buttons can provide an alternative to pinching, though the team must also examine other map operations independently. Make the controls visible, label their purpose, and ensure they produce the same relevant result. A keyboard alternative may help another group of users, but it does not by itself establish that a single-pointer alternative is available.',
        'Test using taps or clicks without dragging, pinching, or tracing a route. Start from the same state, use the gesture and the alternative separately, and compare the outcomes. Verify boundaries, such as the first and last gallery image, and make sure the available control is not hidden behind a gesture. Distinguish path-based gestures from ordinary dragging, which is addressed separately by 2.5.7.'
    ],
    '2.5.7': [
        'Dragging requires a user to press, hold, move to a destination, and release while maintaining control. Someone may be able to tap an item and a destination but have difficulty sustaining a drag or moving precisely. The criterion requires a single-pointer operation without dragging for functionality that uses dragging, unless dragging is essential or the function is controlled by the user agent and not modified by the author.',
        'For a task board, add a Move button or menu on each card. Present the possible destination columns, allow the person to select one by tapping, and give clear feedback after the card moves. If users can reorder within a column by dragging, the alternative must also address that operation; moving between columns alone does not cover reordering. Keyboard support is valuable but is not a substitute for a non-dragging single-pointer method.',
        'Compare the complete operations offered by dragging with the operations offered by the alternative. Use taps to move a card, change its position where supported, and undo or correct a mistake if the application provides that feature. Test touch and mouse without holding down the pointer while moving. Ensure feedback identifies the destination and that the moved item remains discoverable in its new location.'
    ],
    '2.5.8': [
        'A pointer target is the region that responds to a pointer action, not necessarily the visible icon inside it. Small, crowded targets are difficult for users with tremor, limited dexterity, or imprecise touch. The minimum criterion generally uses a 24 by 24 CSS pixel square, subject to specified exceptions. A CSS pixel is a layout unit and should not be assumed to equal a physical display pixel on every screen.',
        'If a target is smaller than the minimum size, the spacing exception can apply when the specified 24-CSS-pixel-diameter circles centered on undersized targets do not intersect another target or the circle of another undersized target. Other exceptions address equivalent controls, inline targets, user-agent control, and essential presentation under their specific conditions. Assess the actual target and its neighbors; a vague claim that there is “enough whitespace” does not demonstrate the spacing rule.',
        'Use browser inspection tools to measure the hit area, then check the nearest neighboring targets and the relevant circles. Increase padding or restructure the layout when controls are crowded, while keeping hit areas from overlapping. Repeat at different viewport sizes and zoom levels. The enhanced AAA target-size criterion generally uses 44 by 44 CSS pixels and has a different set of exceptions, so the minimum spacing rule should not be transferred to it automatically.'
    ]
}

EXPANDED_NOTES.update({
    '2.1.3': [
        'The enhanced keyboard criterion asks for every function to be operable through a keyboard interface without requiring specific timing for individual keystrokes. Unlike the Level A keyboard criterion, it has no exception for an operation whose underlying function depends on a movement path. The difference matters when evaluating a drawing, handwriting, or mapping tool: basic keyboard navigation does not establish that all its functions are available.',
        'Start by inventorying what a pointer user can actually do, including setting coordinates, modifying shapes, deleting elements, and confirming the result. For each operation, design and test an equivalent keyboard workflow. A shape-placement dialog could expose position and size fields and a confirmation button. Check whether it supports the same meaningful operations rather than merely offering an alternative that changes the task.',
        'Test without precisely timed keystrokes. A person should not have to press two keys within a narrow interval merely to complete a normal operation. Verify key instructions, focus location, error recovery, and the final result. If freehand drawing produces unique paths that the keyboard workflow cannot create, document the gap rather than claiming conformance based on a partial alternative.'
    ],
    '2.1.4': [
        'A character key shortcut is triggered by one or more printable characters without a non-printable modifier. It can be useful for rapid navigation, but speech recognition may interpret a dictated character as a command, and a keyboard user may press it while focus is in an unexpected location. The criterion offers safeguards: turn the shortcut off, remap it to include a non-printable modifier, or make it active only when its component has focus.',
        'Consider a video player that uses the letter m for mute. One repair is a preference that disables the shortcut. Another is remapping it to a combination with a modifier; another is limiting the shortcut to the focused player component. Make the chosen mechanism discoverable and operable without triggering the unwanted command. Ensure that an input field elsewhere on the page can receive the letter normally.',
        'Try the shortcut with focus on the player, a neighboring search field, and another page control. If the user disables or changes it, repeat the test after navigating away and returning according to the product’s stated settings behavior. Do not treat a note in documentation as a substitute for one of the specified safeguards. Also check that the new shortcut does not interfere with common browser or assistive technology commands.'
    ],
    '2.2.2': [
        'Moving or updating content competes with a reader’s attention and can make a piece of information disappear before it has been understood. This includes automatic carousels, scrolling announcements, animated content, and live feeds. The criterion distinguishes movement, blinking, or scrolling from auto-updating information, and their conditions are not identical.',
        'For moving, blinking, or scrolling information, examine whether it starts automatically, lasts more than five seconds, and is presented alongside other content. If so, provide a pause, stop, or hide mechanism unless the movement is essential. For auto-updating information that starts automatically and is presented alongside other content, offer pause, stop, hide, or control of update frequency unless essential; the five-second condition does not apply in the same way.',
        'An operable control must change the actual behavior, not merely remove the Pause button. Test by pausing a carousel and waiting longer than its slide interval. For a live feed, verify that updates stop interrupting the displayed information, and make it clear what happens when updates resume. Place controls where users can find and use them with a keyboard. Evaluate seizure-related flashing separately because a pause control does not automatically satisfy flash restrictions.'
    ],
    '2.2.3': [
        'The enhanced timing criterion removes timing as an essential part of the activity except for the stated cases, including non-interactive synchronized media and real-time events. It provides a stronger target than allowing a user to extend an otherwise compulsory deadline. A person working through an ordinary self-paced lesson should be able to pause, consult a resource, and return without losing the chance to complete the activity.',
        'For a practice assessment, separate a timer used for feedback from a deadline that ends the attempt. Showing elapsed time may be useful if it does not block submission or make speed a condition of success. If the task is a genuinely real-time event, identify the reason timing is inherent in the activity rather than citing convenience or a general preference for shorter sessions.',
        'Try beginning the assessment, leaving it idle beyond the previous timeout, returning, editing an answer, and submitting. Verify that a hidden server-side expiration does not negate the visible change. Also check reauthentication and draft preservation where a session may expire independently of the activity. A warning or one extra minute may improve an experience but does not by itself remove timing as an essential part of the task.'
    ],
    '2.2.4': [
        'An interruption is information or an event that diverts the user from their current activity. Unexpected promotional dialogs, chat invitations, and repeated notification sounds can disrupt concentration or cut across assistive technology output. This enhanced criterion requires a way to postpone or suppress interruptions, except interruptions involving an emergency.',
        'A notification setting might let the user turn off promotional prompts or postpone them until the current task is complete. Avoid moving focus to a non-emergency modal while someone is entering data. If the message contains information the person may need later, provide a place to retrieve it rather than silently discarding it. Emergency alerts are treated differently under the criterion, so distinguish their purpose and urgency from ordinary product announcements.',
        'While completing a form, trigger each configured interruption and observe whether focus, typed text, and progress remain stable. Repeat after choosing the postponement or suppression setting. Check the setting with keyboard and screen reader input and confirm that notifications follow the user’s choice. Evaluate visual interruptions and sounds, not only the largest dialog in the design.'
    ],
    '2.2.5': [
        'Authentication expiry may be necessary, but losing work because of it can impose a disproportionate burden on someone who uses assistive technology or needs more time. The criterion addresses the ability to continue an activity without data loss after reauthentication. It applies to the state of the task, not just the ability to reach the sign-in page again.',
        'A multi-step application can save an in-progress draft, request sign-in when the session ends, and return the person to the same step with prior answers intact. Do not automatically submit a partially completed form when the user signs in again. Choose storage and retention carefully: draft data may be sensitive, and returning it to a different person on a shared device would introduce a separate problem.',
        'Create a realistic draft with entries on several steps, allow or simulate session expiry, then sign in again and compare the recovered data. Test validation messages, selected options, uploaded material where applicable, and the current step. Verify that the session is genuinely expired rather than merely showing a mock dialog, and check what happens if the user chooses not to sign in immediately.'
    ],
    '2.2.6': [
        'An inactivity timeout can destroy work even when a person has not left the task voluntarily: they may be reading instructions, using another device, or taking a break. This criterion requires notice of the duration of user inactivity that could cause data loss, unless the data is preserved for more than 20 hours when the user takes no action.',
        'Make the warning specific enough to guide a choice before the loss occurs. “Your unsaved answers will be removed after 15 minutes of inactivity” identifies both duration and consequence. A generic message that a session may expire does not necessarily tell the person what will happen to their work. If the service retains the draft for more than 20 hours, verify the actual retention period rather than assuming that a background save lasts indefinitely.',
        'Compare the warning with the server-side behavior using a safe test draft. Remain inactive for the stated interval, return, and inspect whether the data remains available. If the product changes its timeout or draft-retention policy, update the warning as well. Consider the related reauthentication and timing criteria independently: explaining a data-loss risk is not the same as enabling a user to extend a time limit.'
    ],
    '2.3.1': [
        'Repeated flashes can trigger seizures for some people with photosensitive epilepsy. This risk concerns changes in luminance or saturated red over time, rather than ordinary motion alone. Because a person can be exposed as soon as content is displayed, an optional pause control is not a substitute for evaluating the flashing itself.',
        'At Level A, content must not flash more than three times in any one-second period, or the flash must remain below both the general flash and red flash thresholds. The threshold evaluation depends on technical properties, including the area and characteristics of the flashing content. Use suitable analysis rather than assuming a small or brief animation is harmless from a casual glance.',
        'Audit application animations, videos, advertising, and embedded material that the author controls. The simplest repair for a flashing error banner is often static text and an icon, which still communicate the problem without the effect. When flashing is retained, inspect the rendered sequence using a suitable tool and document the relevant interval and thresholds. The stricter AAA 2.3.2 criterion does not include the below-threshold alternative.'
    ],
    '2.3.2': [
        'This enhanced seizure criterion limits flashes to no more than three in any one-second period. It differs from Level A 2.3.1 because the latter also allows content that remains below both applicable flash thresholds. A small flashing region that meets the Level A threshold alternative can still exceed the rate permitted by this AAA criterion.',
        'Averages across an entire video can hide a short rapid sequence. Examine the interval containing the fastest changes and apply the WCAG definition of a flash when counting. A low overall average does not make a burst of four flashes in a single second acceptable under the enhanced criterion.',
        'Replace an attention-grabbing flash with static emphasis or a non-flashing state change when possible. Inspect authored animation, embedded video, and third-party promotional content in the actual page. If content changes dynamically, test the states users will encounter, not only the first frame. Check separately whether motion triggered by interaction can be disabled under 2.3.3.'
    ],
    '2.3.3': [
        'Motion triggered by a user action can cause dizziness, nausea, or disorientation for people with vestibular sensitivities. Large zooms, parallax effects, and sliding whole panels can be especially problematic. The AAA criterion requires interaction-triggered motion animation to be disableable unless the animation is essential to the functionality or information.',
        'Respecting the operating system reduced-motion preference is a useful implementation technique. For example, a CSS media query can remove a transform transition and show the final state immediately. A site-level setting can provide additional control. The destination content, interaction, and focus behavior must remain available when motion is reduced; disabling the animation should not disable the task.',
        'Enable reduced motion before starting the page, then open menus, switch routes, and display dialogs. Compare the content and controls with the default mode and check for movement introduced by JavaScript or embedded components as well as CSS. If a motion is claimed to be essential, explain what information or functionality would be lost without it instead of treating a decorative transition as essential.'
    ]
})

PRACTICAL_REVIEWS = {
    '2.1.1': 'Review exercise: A sales chart has a button that opens a menu with “Download CSV” and “Download PDF.” Draw a table with one row per action: open menu, move between choices, activate a choice, and dismiss the menu. For each row, record focus position and the key used. If the menu opens on focus but cannot be closed, investigate 2.1.2 as well. If it can be closed but a choice only responds to mouse click, this keyboard criterion is still affected. Use a button for the menu trigger and ensure its expanded state reflects the visible menu; avoid using a clickable div as a shortcut to custom styling.',
    '2.1.2': 'Review exercise: A date picker opens from a calendar button and moves focus to a date. Follow its normal keyboard instructions, then close it without selecting a date. The expected outcome is that the person can continue through the form. If Escape does nothing but a reachable Close button works, test that button before concluding a trap exists. If all keys are consumed and neither control can be reached, record the browser, entry point and key sequence. Restoring focus to the opener is often sensible, but if the opener has disappeared after selection choose another logical place rather than leaving focus nowhere.',
    '2.1.3': 'Review exercise: Compare a pointer-operated map editor with its proposed keyboard alternative. If the pointer lets a user draw an arbitrary boundary but the keyboard interface only selects from three predefined shapes, the alternative does not cover the same capability. A coordinate editor might support editing each boundary point, but it must also support creating and removing points and confirming the result. Test long key sequences at a deliberate pace; a shortcut that requires rapid repeated presses is not a substitute for operations that can be invoked without that timing.',
    '2.1.4': 'Review exercise: A course player uses “k” to pause while a learner writes notes in a separate text field. First test with the player focused, then type “k” into the notes field. If the player pauses while notes are being entered, the shortcut is active too broadly unless another allowed safeguard is in place. Add a disable option, modifier-based remapping, or focus limitation, then retest. Document the chosen safeguard in the product’s keyboard-shortcut help, but remember that the safeguard itself must work; documentation alone does not stop accidental activation.',
    '2.2.1': 'Review exercise: A five-minute application session offers a warning ten seconds before expiry and one extension. This does not satisfy the specified warning-and-extension option: the user must have at least 20 seconds to extend with a simple action, and the extension must be available at least ten times. The team could instead satisfy another allowed option, such as allowing the limit to be turned off before it is encountered. Check whether session expiry actually ends the activity and whether an exception truly applies. Also test that the warning can be perceived and its extension control operated with the user’s input method.',
    '2.2.2': 'Review exercise: A carousel automatically advances every four seconds and sits beside an application form. Add a Pause control, activate it, and wait longer than two slide intervals. The content should stay still until the user chooses otherwise. Next inspect a nearby live feed that changes automatically: a motion-pause button for the carousel does not control feed updates. The feed needs its own suitable pause, stop, hide, or frequency control when the criterion applies. Neither control makes a rapidly flashing animation safe; inspect flashing under the seizure criteria separately.',
    '2.2.3': 'Review exercise: A training quiz awards a score only if the learner submits within two minutes. A ten-minute extension helps but still makes time a condition of completing the ordinary self-paced activity. Remove that condition and preserve answers while the learner reads or takes a break. A visible elapsed-time display may remain if it is informative and does not terminate the task. If a session security timeout still exists, test that reauthentication and restored answers allow the learner to resume. Evaluate whether any claimed real-time exception applies to this specific task.',
    '2.2.4': 'Review exercise: A product shows a sales offer in a modal while the user types an application. Offer a setting to suppress promotions or postpone the message, then trigger the offer while the user is in the middle of a field. Verify that the typed value and focus stay intact and that the choice can be changed later. A genuine emergency warning is treated differently, so the product should not place ordinary marketing messages into the emergency category. The test should include audible as well as visual notifications if both can interrupt the task.',
    '2.2.5': 'Review exercise: Enter a name, choose an option, and type a long explanation in a multi-step application. Let the session expire, sign in as the same user, and compare all three values and the current step. A page that returns the user to the correct step but silently clears the explanation still loses data. Test the case where the user signs in later rather than immediately. The draft must be protected so another person using the same device cannot see it, and it should not be submitted automatically while the user is reauthenticating.',
    '2.2.6': 'Review exercise: A form warns “Your session may expire,” but the server removes unsaved answers after 15 minutes of inactivity. Revise the message to state that period and the potential loss of answers, and place it where the user can encounter it before relying on the form. Test with a safe draft and verify the server’s actual behavior. If the service instead preserves all entered data for more than 20 hours of inactivity, document and test that retention rather than relying on an assumption about browser storage.',
    '2.3.1': 'Review exercise: A red alert alternates with the background four times in one second. Do not assume that it is acceptable simply because it lasts only briefly or provides a Stop button. Replace it with a static alert where possible. If the effect is necessary, examine the rendered sequence with an appropriate flash analysis tool and evaluate both the general and red thresholds in the context of the criterion. Include embedded media the page author controls. Record the tested version and interval so a later animation change can be rechecked.',
    '2.3.2': 'Review exercise: A tiny badge flashes four times in one second and passes the applicable Level A threshold analysis. It can still fail this enhanced criterion because the below-threshold alternative is not present here. Slow or remove the flashing and inspect the most rapid one-second interval. A long video with only one short burst deserves the same examination of that burst; averaging flashes over the complete video could conceal the problem. Test the actual rendered animation rather than inferring its rate from a design mockup.',
    '2.3.3': 'Review exercise: Opening a navigation drawer causes the whole viewport to slide and zoom. Enable the operating system reduced-motion preference before loading the page, then open the drawer. The destination and focus behavior should remain usable even if the transition is removed. Inspect scripts as well as CSS: a JavaScript animation can continue after a CSS transition has been disabled. Test any site-level motion preference separately and confirm that the user can reach it without first triggering a large animation.',
    '2.4.1': 'Review exercise: Add a “Skip to main content” link as the first page control and point it to the main region. Start from the browser address bar, Tab into the page, activate the link, and press Tab again. Verify that the next control is in the main task rather than back in the repeated menu. Check that the target is not obscured by a fixed header. Repeat after navigating to another route, because a fragment target that exists on one page may be missing from another.',
    '2.4.2': 'Review exercise: Open Account, Security, and Billing pages in separate tabs. If all three tabs are called “Dashboard,” rewrite their document titles so the page purpose is distinguishable. Navigate between those screens through client-side links and then use Back and Forward; the title should continue to match the displayed route. Test an error or loading state as well, because route metadata may be missing or stale. A heading in the page body is useful, but a person choosing among browser tabs cannot rely on it.',
    '2.4.3': 'Review exercise: A checkout visually shows shipping, billing, and payment, but CSS places the payment panel first in DOM order. Tab through the task at desktop and narrow widths, writing down each control encountered. Reorder the markup or layout so the sequence preserves meaning. For a dialog opened from the checkout, test focus as it enters, moves within, and leaves the dialog. An arbitrary sequence of positive tabindex values may appear to fix one viewport but can break again when controls are added.',
    '2.4.4': 'Review exercise: A page lists three articles, each followed by a link labeled “Read more.” Inspect how a screen reader determines each link’s context and verify the destinations. If the relationship to the heading is not programmatically available, give each link an informative name such as “Read the enrollment guide.” The Level A criterion may allow programmatically determined context, but purely visual proximity is not proof of that relationship. Also verify that link wording does not promise a different destination from the one actually opened.',
    '2.4.5': 'Review exercise: A help article is reachable from a menu but not returned by site search. Confirm the search index includes the article, search for a likely topic, and open the result with a keyboard. A menu and a search box are only two useful routes if both can reach the intended page. Record the routes for a sample of pages in each documentation section. A page that is a step in or result of a process may have an exception, but an ordinary help page linked from that process is not automatically such a step.',
    '2.4.6': 'Review exercise: A form has three fields labeled “Address” for home, mailing, and billing details. Rename the labels to express each field’s purpose, then inspect the accessible name of every input. Next review a heading list where several sections are called “More information”; replace those headings with their actual topics. The label can be correctly associated and still be misleading, so evaluate its wording as well as the HTML association. Check the resulting labels after translation and when the form dynamically adds fields.',
    '2.4.7': 'Review exercise: A stylesheet sets outline: none on every button and uses a hover color to show interaction. Tab through the interface without moving the mouse: if no control visibly changes, the hover color does not solve the keyboard problem. Add a distinct focus-visible style and check it against both light and dark surfaces. Inspect custom widgets and links as well as buttons. Retest in forced-colors mode and at zoom, where borders or outlines may be clipped by a container.',
    '2.4.8': 'Review exercise: Open a documentation article directly from a search result. A breadcrumb showing “Guides > Forms > Error messages” can explain its location without requiring the user to visit the parent pages first. Check that the parent items navigate to the corresponding sections and that the current item is correctly identified. A menu highlight can be another cue, but it must be visible and understandable when the page opens directly. Avoid showing a misleading breadcrumb based on a route that is not part of the site hierarchy.',
    '2.4.9': 'Review exercise: Open a screen reader links list for a page containing several “Download” links. If each downloads a different report, change the link names to identify the reports, such as “Download the 2025 accessibility report.” Verify that the visible label and accessible name remain compatible for voice users. Unlike the Level A link-purpose criterion, this enhanced criterion calls for purpose to be available from link text alone, subject to the general-ambiguity exception; surrounding table cells should not be assumed to solve every repeated link name.',
    '2.4.10': 'Review exercise: A long cancellation policy contains paragraphs about eligibility, refunds, and appeals but has no section headings. Add headings that describe those subjects, use appropriate semantic heading levels, and inspect the resulting outline. Ask a reader to find the appeal process using only the headings before checking the full text. A heading that says “More” for every section is not an effective navigation aid. Avoid introducing a heading solely to obtain a larger font when it does not represent a section.',
    '2.4.11': 'Review exercise: With a sticky footer visible, Tab to a submit button near the bottom of a form. If the entire button is hidden, change the layout or scroll positioning to expose it and repeat at a short viewport height. Test with the notification banner open as well as closed, because multiple overlays can combine. At this AA level inspect whether the component is entirely obscured; the enhanced criterion requires the whole component to remain visible. Check focus-indicator visibility as a related but separate requirement.',
    '2.4.12': 'Review exercise: A sticky header covers the top half of a focused link. Although part of the link remains visible, this enhanced criterion requires the entire focused component to be unobscured by author-created content. Add layout space or a suitable scroll offset, then retest at high zoom and in a translated interface where the header wraps. Check both top and bottom overlays and the whole component boundary, not just the visible text. The fix should continue to work when a banner expands dynamically.',
    '2.4.13': 'Review exercise: A button uses a thin blue border in both states, changing only to a slightly darker blue on focus. Capture the same area before and after focus. Check whether enough pixels meet the required 3:1 state-change contrast and whether their area meets the two-pixel-perimeter equivalent. A border that contrasts against the page background is not necessarily sufficient if it barely changes on focus. Inspect clipping and overlapping elements, and assess the criterion’s exceptions before reporting the result.',
    '2.5.1': 'Review exercise: A gallery changes photos by swiping, but its arrow controls are decorative and do not respond to a tap. Convert them into operable previous and next buttons and verify they reach the same images as the swipe. Test with a single pointer without tracing a path. For a pinch-to-zoom image, zoom buttons can offer another route to magnification. A keyboard-only workaround can help some users but does not replace the required single-pointer alternative when this criterion applies.',
    '2.5.2': 'Review exercise: A user presses a Delete button, notices the mistake, moves the pointer away, and releases. The record should not be deleted if the chosen design uses release with an abort mechanism. If the action is performed on pointer down, examine whether the up event reverses it or another permitted condition applies. Test using disposable sample records. A visually displayed Undo control is not sufficient unless it actually restores the affected data after normal activation.',
    '2.5.3': 'Review exercise: A visible button says “Search,” but aria-label gives it the accessible name “Find products.” Inspect the computed name, then change it to include “Search,” for example “Search products.” A speech user should be able to identify the control using the visible wording. Repeat after switching languages and after the button label changes during a loading state. Avoid hiding necessary visible label words inside unrelated programmatic names, even if those names sound descriptive in isolation.',
    '2.5.4': 'Review exercise: Shaking a phone undoes the last action. Add an Undo button and a preference to turn off the shake response. With motion disabled, shake the phone and confirm nothing is undone; then use the button and verify that it performs the same undo. Check that the preference is accessible without shaking. If the team claims movement is essential, ask what function would be impossible through an alternative control and record the justification for the actual feature.',
    '2.5.5': 'Review exercise: A toolbar icon is 16 by 16 CSS pixels, but its button has padding that makes the target 44 by 44. Measure the actual hit area, not just the icon. Verify that adjacent targets do not overlap and that the toolbar remains usable at zoom. An inline link within a sentence may be subject to a specific exception; a standalone toolbar button should not be assumed to qualify for the same reason. Also distinguish the enhanced criterion’s exceptions from the smaller AA minimum-size criterion.',
    '2.5.6': 'Review exercise: On a laptop with a touchscreen, open a menu by touch, enter text using the keyboard, and select another control with a trackpad. The task should continue without reloading or losing input because the first interaction was touch. Inspect third-party widgets if the rest of the page supports switching but one component does not. Review any security or user-setting restriction against the specific exception instead of treating touchscreen detection itself as a reason to disable keyboard support.',
    '2.5.7': 'Review exercise: A task board allows a card to be dragged between columns and reordered within a column. Add a single-pointer Move menu and include both the destination and position controls necessary to reproduce those operations. Tap through the alternative without holding and moving the pointer. Compare the final state with the drag result, including any status announcements. Arrow-key support is valuable for keyboard users, but it does not by itself provide a non-dragging single-pointer operation.',
    '2.5.8': 'Review exercise: Two 16-by-16 icon buttons sit directly beside each other. Enlarge their hit areas to at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels or assess the precise spacing exception using centered 24-pixel-diameter circles and neighboring targets. Measure the clickable region rather than the visible icon and ensure that enlarged regions do not overlap. Check inline links and user-agent controls against their actual exception conditions. At AAA the 44-by-44 enhanced target criterion has different requirements and exceptions.'
}

IMPLEMENTATION_REVIEW = {
    '2.1.1': 'Implementation review: For an action, replace <div onclick="save()">Save</div> with <button type="button">Save</button> and attach the same save operation to its click event. A native button provides keyboard focus and standard activation; do not add a second keydown handler that causes Enter or Space to save twice. Tab to the button, activate with Enter, then repeat with Space. Check the actual saved result, not merely whether the button receives focus. If save opens a confirmation, carry the keyboard test through confirmation and back to the form. For a link that navigates, use an anchor with a meaningful href rather than choosing a button merely because both can receive focus.',
    '2.1.2': 'Implementation review: Suppose a dialog intercepts Tab to keep focus inside while open. Test the first and last focusable controls in both directions, then use Escape or an operable Close button. A loop within an active dialog is not enough to prove a trap: the decisive question is whether the user can leave using the keyboard. If no standard exit is supported, instructions must describe the method needed to leave. After closing, check that focus returns to the opener or another meaningful position and that the next Tab reaches the surrounding task. Also test a nested embedded widget, whose key handling may prevent the dialog exit from working.',
    '2.1.3': 'Implementation review: A canvas diagram tool exposes Insert rectangle, X position, Y position, width, height, and Delete as keyboard controls. Walk through creation, modification, and deletion without a pointer. Then compare with the pointer feature set: if users can draw arbitrary curves but keyboard users can only insert rectangles, the coverage is incomplete. The stronger criterion has no path-dependent exception, so record unsupported curve operations rather than assuming the baseline exception carries over. Check that every coordinate field is labeled and that error correction does not require rapid key sequences. A visible toolbar and focusable canvas alone do not demonstrate equivalent functionality.',
    '2.1.4': 'Implementation review: In a media component, a keydown listener for m can check whether the relevant player has focus before toggling mute. Alternatively, settings can disable the character shortcut or remap it with a non-printable modifier. Open a separate text field and type the character: audio should not change when the focus-based safeguard is used. Test the focused player as well, so the repair does not inadvertently remove intended behavior. If a setting is offered, use the control itself with keyboard input and verify its saved state as promised by the product. A help page explaining the shortcut is not one of the required safeguards.',
    '2.2.1': 'Implementation review: A form expires after five minutes. A warning appears at four minutes forty seconds and provides an Extend button. Time the interval in the actual application: it must provide at least 20 seconds to respond for this option, use a simple action, and allow at least ten extensions. A warning shown after expiry cannot provide that opportunity. If the application instead permits turning the limit off or adjusting it to at least ten times the default before it is encountered, test that chosen path. Preserve sample answers while testing and document whether the real-time, essential, or longer-than-20-hour exception actually applies.',
    '2.2.2': 'Implementation review: A carousel advances on a timer. Its Pause button should stop the timer or otherwise stop the actual movement, update its own label or state appropriately, and remain keyboard operable. Activate Pause and observe for longer than the normal interval; merely hiding the slide does not show the motion has stopped. A live feed next to the carousel is a separate auto-updating item and needs an appropriate control if the criterion applies. Test each source independently. Do not apply the carousel’s five-second condition to conclude that a rapidly updating feed needs no user control.',
    '2.2.3': 'Implementation review: A practice quiz originally rejects submissions after 60 seconds. Remove that server-side rejection as well as the visual countdown so users can finish at their own pace. Retaining an elapsed-time display for self-reflection is different from requiring a submission deadline. Test by pausing longer than the original limit and submitting a changed answer. If authentication expires, check that sign-in and restored data still permit completion. An extension that remains compulsory improves the experience but does not remove timing as an essential part of an ordinary self-paced activity.',
    '2.2.4': 'Implementation review: A notification preference can suppress promotional popups while a person fills in a form. Trigger the promotion while focus is in a text field and verify that no modal steals focus when suppression is enabled. If postponement is offered, verify the notification can be found later and that the user can change the preference. Test nonvisual interruptions such as notification sounds as well as modal dialogs. An emergency alert is handled by the exception; the team should not classify routine advertising as an emergency merely to bypass a user preference.',
    '2.2.5': 'Implementation review: Save an unfinished application as a protected draft keyed to the authenticated user. When the session expires, prompt for authentication and restore the same draft and current task afterward. Test with several distinct values so partial loss is visible. Check files and multi-step selections when the product supports them; restoring the first field alone is not enough. Do not submit automatically after login. On a shared computer, verify that a different account cannot retrieve the draft, and apply the product’s retention policy consistently. A user may still need notice about inactivity-related loss under 2.2.6.',
    '2.2.6': 'Implementation review: A form that deletes unsaved entries after 15 minutes should disclose that duration and consequence before the person risks loss. Compare the displayed message with server behavior using a test draft and an actual inactivity interval. A message about automatic sign-out is incomplete if it does not explain that entered answers will be lost. If data is preserved for more than 20 hours while the user takes no action, verify that retention rather than assuming a browser cache persists. Keep the disclosure in sync when the timeout or data-retention policy changes.',
    '2.3.1': 'Implementation review: Replace a rapidly alternating red error banner with static text, an error icon, and a clear heading. The information remains available without requiring a visual flash to attract attention. If a flash is retained, analyze the rendered content over the relevant one-second interval and evaluate the applicable general and red flash thresholds with suitable tools. A small apparent area, a short clip, or an optional pause button alone does not establish compliance. Repeat analysis when an embedded video or animation is edited; a safe still image says nothing about the sequence of changes over time.',
    '2.3.2': 'Implementation review: A four-flashes-per-second badge may qualify under a threshold alternative at Level A yet still fail this enhanced criterion, which does not include that alternative. Remove or slow the flash, then inspect the fastest one-second interval of the rendered sequence. Do not average the total flashes over a long video: a brief burst can exceed the allowed rate even when the overall average looks low. Review third-party content controlled by the page author as well as the site’s own animation. A user-controlled pause is not a replacement for evaluating the rate.',
    '2.3.3': 'Implementation review: A drawer that slides across the screen can use a reduced-motion media query to replace its transform transition with an immediate state change. Turn on the preference before opening the drawer, and verify that its controls and focus behavior remain available. Inspect JavaScript-driven animations too: disabling CSS transition does not stop a script that moves the element frame by frame. If a product offers a separate motion setting, ensure it is reachable without triggering the animation first. Record any claimed essential exception with a reason tied to the information or function.',
    '2.4.1': 'Implementation review: Put <a href="#main">Skip to main content</a> near the beginning of the page and provide a main region with the matching id. If the target needs programmatic focus for the supported browsers, implement and test that behavior. Starting from browser chrome, Tab to the link, activate it, and press Tab again. The user should reach the main task without traversing the repeated navigation. A fixed header should not cover the target. Repeat on each route: a correct link with a missing or duplicate target on one page does not provide a reliable bypass there.',
    '2.4.2': 'Implementation review: Set an initial title such as “Payment details – Example Store” and update document.title after client-side navigation. Open payment, shipping, and account screens in separate tabs and identify them from the tab labels. Then navigate with Back and Forward and verify the title changes with the displayed route. A route that fails to update the title can leave a user believing they are still on the previous screen. Check error and loading routes for missing title data. The visible heading is also useful, but a browser-tab user needs the document title.',
    '2.4.3': 'Implementation review: Keep fields in a meaningful DOM order and use layout CSS without creating an unrelated keyboard sequence. Tab through the form while recording the order, then reverse with Shift+Tab. Repeat after a narrow viewport stacks columns and after validation inserts an error. When a dialog opens, move focus to an appropriate location inside and return it logically on close. A jump to a footer between related form fields can disrupt the task even if every control is individually reachable. Avoid positive tabindex as a fragile patch for an underlying source-order problem.',
    '2.4.4': 'Implementation review: Replace an isolated <a href="/refund">Click here</a> with a link whose text identifies the destination, such as “Read the refund policy.” If a design retains shorter link labels, test whether the permitted programmatic context is actually available to assistive technology. Merely placing a heading visually near a link is not proof of association. Compare the announced purpose with the destination and check repeated labels that point to different pages. At this level context can be permitted; the enhanced link-purpose criterion asks for purpose from link text alone subject to its exception.',
    '2.4.5': 'Implementation review: Make a documentation article reachable from a topic menu and from site search. Search for its topic using realistic words, open the result, and confirm it is the same page. An empty search index does not create a second route just because a search field is visible. A site map or meaningful contextual navigation can provide another path depending on the site. Sample pages across sections and note any with only one route. Evaluate the process-step exception for the specific page rather than assuming every page in an application qualifies.',
    '2.4.6': 'Implementation review: Give an email input a persistent label such as <label for="email">Email address</label> and connect it to the matching input id. Inspect the computed name and check that it describes what the user should enter. A correctly associated label reading “Details” can still be too vague. Review headings in the same way: “Payment method” is more useful than a repeated “More.” Test dynamically added fields and localized versions for broken associations or misleading duplicate labels. Placeholder text that disappears on entry should not serve as the sole persistent label.',
    '2.4.7': 'Implementation review: Replace a global outline: none rule with a visible keyboard focus style, for example a sufficiently distinct outline on :focus-visible. Tab through links, buttons, form fields, and custom widgets on light and dark surfaces. A hover-only color does not reveal keyboard focus, and a selected menu item is not necessarily the focused item. Check that the ring is not clipped by overflow or covered by an adjacent component. Inspect the result in forced-colors mode and at zoom. The enhanced appearance criterion adds separate measurement requirements.',
    '2.4.8': 'Implementation review: A breadcrumb such as “Guides > Forms > Error messages” can identify the current article’s position in the documentation set. Use actual parent links and mark the current page programmatically when appropriate, for example with aria-current="page". Open the article directly from search and check that the cue still makes sense. Test the links with keyboard and a screen reader. A route title can identify the current page while failing to communicate where it sits among related pages, so inspect the hierarchy cue separately.',
    '2.4.9': 'Implementation review: A links list containing “Download,” “Download,” and “Download” does not distinguish three reports. Give the links names such as “Download the accessibility audit” and “Download the remediation plan.” Review those names without their surrounding paragraphs and compare each destination. If additional accessible text is used, keep the visible label in the computed name so speech users can invoke it. At this enhanced level, surrounding programmatically determined context should not be treated as a substitute for link text that identifies the purpose, subject to the criterion’s general-ambiguity exception.',
    '2.4.10': 'Implementation review: Divide a long policy into sections headed “Eligibility,” “How to apply,” and “Appeals,” using semantic heading elements in a coherent hierarchy. Inspect the heading list separately from the page body, then verify that each heading accurately describes the following content. Styling a paragraph in bold can create a visual cue but may not expose the same structure to assistive technology. Conversely, using headings just for larger font can make the outline misleading. Test nested sections and translated versions as content changes.',
    '2.4.11': 'Implementation review: When focus lands on a button behind a sticky footer, adjust the layout or scrolling so the component is not entirely hidden. Test at a short viewport height, at zoom, and with optional banners open. A fixed scroll offset can fail if an overlay grows or wraps; inspect the actual rendered page. This AA criterion concerns a component entirely obscured by author-created content, while the enhanced criterion requires the whole component to remain visible. Test the focus indicator separately, because seeing part of a component does not necessarily make its indicator visible.',
    '2.4.12': 'Implementation review: A fixed header that covers the top edge of a focused field creates a partial obstruction. Reserve adequate space or change the scroll positioning, then inspect all edges of the focused component. Repeat with zoom, a narrow viewport, translated header text, and a virtual keyboard when relevant. An offset chosen for a single header height may fail when the header wraps. This AAA requirement is stricter than the AA criterion that addresses a component entirely hidden. Verify that correcting the header does not leave the component covered by a footer instead.',
    '2.4.13': 'Implementation review: Capture a control before and after focus at the same scale. Determine the area of pixels constituting the focus indicator and compare the same pixels across the two states for the required 3:1 change. The qualifying area must meet the two-CSS-pixel-perimeter equivalent described by the criterion. Contrast with a neighboring background is a different measurement and does not automatically prove state-change contrast. Include clipping and overlap in the inspection, and evaluate applicable exceptions for user-agent-determined or author-unmodified indicators. Test representative component shapes and themes.',
    '2.5.1': 'Implementation review: A gallery with swipe navigation can provide native Previous and Next buttons. Tap each button with a single pointer and confirm it reaches the same images as the swipe. For pinch zoom, plus and minus controls can expose magnification without a multipoint gesture; assess other operations independently. A button reachable only through a keyboard shortcut does not provide the required single-pointer alternative. If a path or multipoint gesture is claimed to be essential, explain why the underlying function cannot be performed through an alternative rather than relying on the original design choice.',
    '2.5.2': 'Implementation review: Use a normal release-based activation for a destructive action and allow the user to move away before release, or provide another mechanism permitted by the criterion. Test by pressing, holding, moving outside, and releasing over safe sample data. Then activate normally and test any Undo. Inspect pointerdown handlers as well as click handlers: an early deletion cannot be cured by an unrelated release handler unless the criterion’s conditions are actually met. Consider the essential exception in relation to the specific operation, not all controls in a class.',
    '2.5.3': 'Implementation review: A visible “Search” button with aria-label="Find products" can be hard to activate by speech using the visible word. Give it an accessible name containing the visible text, such as “Search products,” and inspect the computed name in browser tools. Test after translation and when the visible label changes. Avoid accidentally overriding meaningful button text with an unrelated aria-label or aria-labelledby reference. The visible wording at the beginning of the accessible name is a helpful convention; the requirement is that the accessible name contains the visible label under the criterion’s conditions.',
    '2.5.4': 'Implementation review: For shake-to-undo, provide an Undo button and a setting that disables the shake response. Use the button without moving the device and verify that it undoes the same operation. Then disable the motion response, shake the device, and confirm no accidental undo occurs. Both tests matter because the alternative control and the ability to disable motion address different barriers. Evaluate the supported-interface and essential exceptions against the actual feature. A motion gesture that is merely fun or convenient is not automatically essential.',
    '2.5.5': 'Implementation review: An icon can remain visually 16 by 16 CSS pixels while padding gives its button a 44 by 44 CSS pixel hit area. Measure the interactive region and test activation at its edges; do not measure only the graphic. Check neighboring targets for overlaps and test at zoom. If the target is smaller, examine the specific enhanced-criterion exceptions, such as an eligible equivalent target or inline presentation, rather than borrowing the AA spacing exception. Record which exception applies and verify its conditions on the actual page.',
    '2.5.6': 'Implementation review: Do not disable keyboard event handling merely because a touchscreen is detected. On a hybrid device, open a menu by touch, enter text with a keyboard, and activate another control with a pointer during the same task. Confirm focus and entered data are retained when switching. Repeat in embedded widgets, which may use different input handling. If the product restricts a modality for an essential function, security, or a user setting, document the specific reason and verify that the restriction is limited to that case.',
    '2.5.7': 'Implementation review: A draggable task card can also expose a Move menu with choices for destination and position. Tap the card’s menu, select a column, and choose an ordering position without holding and moving a pointer. Compare the result with the drag workflow. If dragging permits reordering but the menu only changes columns, the alternative covers only part of the functionality. Keyboard commands are useful but do not replace the single-pointer non-dragging operation. Inspect whether any essential or user-agent-controlled exception applies to the particular functionality.',
    '2.5.8': 'Implementation review: Measure the clickable bounds of an icon button in CSS pixels. If it cannot contain a 24 by 24 CSS pixel square, assess the exact spacing exception by checking the required centered 24-pixel-diameter circle against neighboring targets and circles for other undersized targets. Enlarging padding may be simpler, but verify that hit regions do not overlap. Check inline links, equivalent targets, and other exceptions according to their stated conditions. The AAA target criterion generally uses 44 by 44 and has different exceptions, so passing the minimum rule does not automatically establish the enhanced one.'
}

EXPANDED_NOTES.update({
    '2.5.2': [
        'Pointer cancellation helps prevent accidental activation when someone presses the wrong target. An action performed immediately on pointer down can leave no opportunity to move away before committing it. This is especially consequential for deletion, cancellation, and purchases, but even a less serious action can disrupt a person’s task.',
        'The criterion provides several permitted approaches: no part of the function is executed on the down event; the action completes on up and there is a mechanism to abort before completion or undo afterward; the up event reverses the down-event outcome; or completing on down is essential. Evaluate the actual event handling. A click handler does not help if another pointerdown handler has already carried out the irreversible operation.',
        'With safe sample data, press and hold the target, move outside it, and release. Observe whether the action is aborted or whether an available undo restores the previous state. Then perform a normal activation and verify its result. Test touch and mouse where supported, and inspect the essential-function exception in the context of the actual control rather than assuming it applies to all buttons.'
    ],
    '2.5.3': [
        'A person using speech input often tries to activate a control by saying its visible label. If its accessible name uses different words, the command may fail even though the control looks correct. The visible and programmatic names also matter when one person helps another navigate a page using the words displayed on screen.',
        'For an interface component with a visible text label or image of text, the accessible name must contain the visible text. Place that text at the beginning when practical, followed by useful context. For example, a button visibly labeled “Search” might have the accessible name “Search products.” Be careful when adding aria-label or aria-labelledby, because these attributes can override the name that would otherwise come from the button’s text.',
        'Inspect the computed accessible name rather than assuming it matches the markup. Compare it with the text visible on screen, and try invoking the control using that wording with speech software where available. Repeat for translated labels, icon buttons with visible text, and states in which a label changes. A descriptive accessible name that omits the visible words can still create a speech-input barrier.'
    ],
    '2.5.4': [
        'Some interfaces perform actions when a device is moved or when a person makes a motion detected by a sensor. Examples include shaking a phone to undo or using a camera-detected gesture. Someone may be unable to perform the motion, while another person may trigger it unintentionally during ordinary movement.',
        'Provide an alternative user-interface component for the operation and let the user disable motion response, except where an applicable supported-interface or essential exception applies. For shake-to-undo, a visible Undo button offers another way to perform the action; a preference to turn off shake detection addresses accidental triggers. These are separate needs, so adding only one of the two may leave a barrier.',
        'Perform the same operation without moving the device, then disable motion activation and check that ordinary movement no longer invokes it. Verify the preference can itself be reached and operated. Consider whether motion is genuinely essential to the function or whether it is simply a convenient shortcut. Document the reason when relying on an exception.'
    ],
    '2.5.5': [
        'A target is the interactive area that responds to pointer input. An icon may look small while its surrounding clickable area is larger, so assess the actual hit area rather than only the painted graphic. The enhanced AAA criterion generally requires a target of at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels, subject to its specified exceptions.',
        'Larger targets help users whose pointer movement is imprecise and reduce accidental activation of neighboring controls. Padding can enlarge a button without changing the apparent size of its icon, but ensure that neighboring hit areas do not overlap. Review the actual layout when text wraps or the interface is zoomed. Do not substitute the spacing exception from the AA minimum-target criterion for an exception in this enhanced criterion.',
        'Measure each target with browser tools and activate it near its edges with a pointer or touch device. Check closely spaced toolbar controls and links in prose separately because the criterion includes an inline exception with specific conditions. If relying on an equivalent target, verify that it is on the same page and provides the same function under the criterion’s conditions.'
    ],
    '2.5.6': [
        'Modern devices often support more than one input method at the same time. A person may use touch for large targets, a keyboard to enter text, and a mouse for precise placement. An interface that detects a touchscreen and disables keyboard or mouse handling can prevent the user from switching methods as their needs or task change.',
        'The enhanced concurrent-input criterion says content must not restrict available platform input modalities except where a restriction is essential, necessary for content security, or required to respect user settings. Supporting touch should ordinarily add an input option rather than replacing other available methods. Review application scripts and embedded components because either can impose an unexpected restriction.',
        'On a hybrid device, start a task by touch, enter information with a keyboard, and use a pointer for the next control without reloading. Verify that focus, entered values, and state are retained. Then switch in the other direction. If a restriction is claimed to be essential or security-related, document the specific function and reason instead of relying on the fact that the device has a touchscreen.'
    ]
})

EXPANDED_NOTES.update({
    '2.4.1': [
        'Repeated blocks can include a site-wide navigation menu, search area, or other content appearing before the main part of many pages. A keyboard user who must move through every repeated link on each visit spends time before reaching the task. A bypass mechanism provides a shorter route; the requirement concerns repeated blocks rather than demanding a particular visual design for every page.',
        'A common implementation places a skip link near the start of the document and links it to the main region. Make it visible when focused so a keyboard user can discover it, and ensure the target exists. A target may need to receive programmatic focus to give a reliable keyboard experience in the supported environment. Landmarks or other mechanisms can also help, depending on how users access the page.',
        'Starting at the browser chrome, Tab into the page and activate the bypass mechanism. Check where the page scrolls, where focus lands, and what happens on the next Tab. A sticky header must not obscure the destination. Repeat on representative pages with shared navigation and after client-side route changes. A link that only looks like a skip control but has a broken fragment target does not provide the intended route.'
    ],
    '2.4.2': [
        'The document title appears in browser tabs, history, bookmarks, and assistive technology announcements. It provides context before someone examines the body of the page. Titles that are blank or repeatedly say “Home” make it hard to distinguish a payment form from an account page when several tabs are open.',
        'Describe the actual topic or purpose first, followed by supporting site context when useful: “Payment details – Example Store” is more informative than “Example Store.” In a single-page application, the browser may keep the same document loaded while the visible route changes, so update document.title as part of navigation. A title should reflect the current state, not just the name of the application shell.',
        'Open several representative pages in separate tabs and identify each using only its title. Navigate between routes using links and the browser Back button and verify that the title tracks the visible page. Review dynamically generated titles for missing data and duplicated separators. A meaningful heading inside the page remains useful, but it does not replace a descriptive document title.'
    ],
    '2.4.4': [
        'Link purpose tells a person where a link goes or what it will do. Some users read links in a list, while others encounter each link within its paragraph or table. The Level A criterion permits the purpose to be determined from the link text alone or from link text together with programmatically determined context, except when the purpose would be ambiguous to users in general.',
        'Use direct wording such as “Read the refund policy” when possible. Repeated “Read more” links may be understandable visually because headings appear nearby, but proximity alone does not prove that assistive technology can determine the association. If relying on context, inspect the actual structure and announcement in the relevant reading mode. Avoid misleading labels where different destinations use indistinguishable names.',
        'Review a list of articles and record the text or accessible name of every link and its destination. Ask whether the purpose is clear in the relevant programmatic context. Test a screen reader as well as visual presentation, and check links that open files or external resources. The enhanced 2.4.9 criterion is stricter about link purpose from link text alone, so do not conflate the two levels.'
    ],
    '2.4.5': [
        'People may search for a page directly, browse categories, follow a site map, or use contextual links. A single navigation path assumes they understand the site’s structure in the same way as its authors. This criterion requires more than one way to locate a page within a set of web pages, except when the page is a step in or result of a process.',
        'A documentation site might make a help article reachable through both a topic menu and search. A site map or a meaningful set of related links may serve a similar purpose. The methods must actually locate the intended page: a search field that excludes the article from its index is not a working second route. The process exception should be evaluated for the specific page, not applied to an entire application merely because it contains forms.',
        'Choose representative pages across sections and follow at least two routes to each. Test search with realistic terms and verify the result opens the correct destination. Use keyboard navigation for each route and check that permission settings do not unexpectedly hide one route from the intended audience. Record pages with only one practical route and decide whether the process exception genuinely applies.'
    ],
    '2.4.6': [
        'A heading describes the topic of a section, while a label identifies the purpose of an input or other control. Both help people decide what to read or enter. Vague labels such as “Information” repeated across different form fields increase the chance of mistakes and make assistive technology navigation less useful.',
        'Choose wording that describes the actual task: “Delivery address” distinguishes that field from “Billing address,” and “Refund eligibility” is more informative than “Details.” Associate labels with their form controls using the appropriate HTML relationship. A visible placeholder is not a durable replacement for a label because it can disappear once the user enters a value and may not provide the required association.',
        'Review headings in isolation as a page outline and inspect form fields with their accessible names. Compare each heading with the section that follows and each label with the expected input. Test with both visual inspection and a screen reader or accessibility tree. A programmatically associated label can still be inadequate if its wording fails to describe the field’s purpose.'
    ],
    '2.4.8': [
        'A page may be understandable on its own while still leaving a user uncertain where it sits in a larger site. Someone arriving from a search result may not know which documentation topic contains the current article or how to return to its parent section. This enhanced criterion requires information about the user’s location within a set of web pages.',
        'A breadcrumb can show the path from a broader category to the current page. A navigation menu that clearly indicates the current item can also communicate location, depending on the structure. Use the actual hierarchy, not an invented sequence based only on the path the last user happened to take. Where appropriate, expose the current page programmatically, for example with aria-current.',
        'Open an interior page directly, as if following a search result, and determine whether the location cue identifies both the current page and its relationship to the wider site. Test the parent links and the current-item announcement with keyboard and assistive technology. A descriptive browser title and a page heading help identify the page but do not necessarily explain its position in the site hierarchy.'
    ],
    '2.4.9': [
        'The enhanced link-purpose criterion asks that the purpose of each link be identifiable from link text alone, except where its purpose would be ambiguous to users in general. This differs from the Level A criterion, which can permit programmatically determined context. The distinction is useful when a screen reader user reviews a list of links outside their surrounding paragraphs.',
        'A list containing multiple “Read more” links forces the user to return to the surrounding content to determine which article each link opens. Replace them with names such as “Read the registration guide” and “Read the cancellation policy.” If adding visually hidden text or an accessible name, ensure that the visible label is still included in the accessible name where required for speech input.',
        'Generate a links list, read each entry without its paragraph, and compare it with the destination. Check whether duplicate names lead to different places and whether the names meaningfully distinguish them. Review dynamic lists after data changes and after translation. Avoid adding long, repetitive prefixes that make scanning harder while trying to add context.'
    ],
    '2.4.10': [
        'Section headings expose the organization of written content. They support people who scan before reading, readers who need to revisit a particular instruction, and screen reader users who navigate by headings. The criterion concerns headings that organize content; it does not mean every paragraph needs a heading or that larger text alone creates a useful structure.',
        'Divide a long policy into meaningful sections such as eligibility, application steps, and appeals. Use a heading that accurately describes each section and apply semantic heading elements with levels that reflect the document hierarchy. Avoid choosing a heading level solely for its visual size; styling can be adjusted separately from structure.',
        'Inspect the heading list without reading the body. Can a learner predict where to find a refund rule or a required document? Then read each section to verify that its heading remains accurate. Check nested sections, dynamically loaded content, and translated pages. Related criteria address whether headings and labels describe their purpose; this criterion focuses on providing headings that organize the content.'
    ],
    '2.4.12': [
        'A focused control can be partially covered by a fixed header, a cookie notice, or a floating panel. A person may see part of it but miss its label, value, or surrounding context. This AAA criterion requires that no part of the focused component be hidden by author-created content, a stricter standard than the AA condition addressing a component that is entirely hidden.',
        'The problem can depend on viewport size, zoom, or content wrapping. A banner that is shallow on a wide screen may become tall when zoomed or translated. Provide sufficient layout space or adjust scrolling so the focused component remains fully visible. A hard-coded offset must be tested against the actual size of the overlay rather than assumed to work in every state.',
        'Tab to controls near fixed regions at several viewport heights and zoom levels. Inspect all edges of the component, not only whether the focus indicator is visible. Test with notices expanded and collapsed and with a virtual keyboard where relevant. Confirm that a fix for one overlay does not cause another to cover the next focused control.'
    ],
    '2.4.13': [
        'An indicator that is technically present can still be too thin or too similar to the unfocused state to distinguish. The enhanced focus appearance criterion adds measurable requirements for the size of the indicator and the contrast change between focused and unfocused states, subject to stated exceptions. It is not enough to check whether a control has any outline property in CSS.',
        'The qualifying area must be at least the area of a two-CSS-pixel-thick perimeter of the unfocused component or subcomponent, and the indicator must achieve a contrast change of at least 3:1 between the same pixels in the two states. Distinguish this state comparison from the contrast between adjacent colors. A bright focus ring may contrast with its neighbor but still require checking against what occupied those same pixels before focus.',
        'Measure the rendered component and compare focused and unfocused screenshots or use an appropriate inspection tool. Account for clipping, overlays, and parts of an indicator that are not visible. Inspect custom controls, different themes, and zoomed layouts. Check the specified exceptions for user-agent-determined or unmodified indicators rather than assuming all browser defaults are handled identically.'
    ]
})
